"In Java, Throwable is the parent class for both Error and Exception.
Errors generally represent serious JVM or system-level problems,
 such as OutOfMemoryError or StackOverflowError,
 and are not normally handled by application code.
 Exceptions represent conditions that an application can
 often anticipate and handle using try-catch.
 Exceptions are further divided into checked exceptions
and unchecked exceptions, with RuntimeException being
 the parent of common unchecked exceptions such as
 ArithmeticException and NullPointerException.


 An unchecked exception is an exception that the compiler does not force you to handle or declare.
 A checked exception ie subclasses of RuntimeException is an exception that Java's compiler forces
 you to handle or declare